ar was risen the  standards standards appropriate standard  do you think that was the beginning of  the end for horiz the match I think I  think it was a range of things to be  honest it wasn't just the one incident  appalling though it was it was a range  of things that we went on to see in  terms of Standards conduct the culture  the whole culture of the Mets misogyny  and obviously that's been subject to  plenty of reports there's a new Chief  now that's obviously a new commissioner  that's overseeing the change that's  required  and you do the consultation and you get  all these hundreds of thousands of  responsive you add in the ones from  before as well  and as a result uh you put 25 million to  safer Street funds you have the five  million safety for women night fund was  it ever enough is that is it ever enough  in terms of what you can do  I think it's it's more than money if I  may say so these funds are really  important safer Street funds I'm a big  champion and advocate of that around the  country they're doing great things to  give women confidence this is the key  thing that they should be free and open  to live their lives go out in the  evenings with their friends Etc that's  really really important and I think that  must absolutely continue I think the  other point about this is It's the  public awareness I think we've changed  attitudes we've raised awareness you  know we want to give people confidence  that they can come forward seek support  but I think importantly but you know I  say this as a mother as a woman as well  we want to live our lives freely and  openly right and not feel the threat of  harassment have you ever experienced on  a personal level  harassment or abuse  you have can you so I as a politician  I've been a member of Parham for 12  years over the last 12 years I've had  consistent levels of I think harassment  and abuse so harassments is just  intimidation  unkind things that obviously said to me  I have people try and follow me home  actually as well so all of that is  unacceptable the abuse much of it's  documented some of it is documented even  in Parliament you know conduct of some  MPS towards one another and myself in  particular people saying that I have no  right to speak out on certain issues  because of the color of my skin because  of my politics for example that's  unacceptable I've had a lot of death  threats cases have gone to court that is  all simply unacceptable does whatever  make you think is it worth being in the  public eye if I'm getting death threats  and being abused like that  it provides pools for thought there's no  doubt about that but I came into  politics I believe in public service I'm  very old-fashioned in that sense Beth I  do believe in public service in terms of  Vince Gates won't grow the rate review  uh they set the rate review in 2021 set  targets to get charge levels back and  prosecution levels back to rates seen in  2016-17 that's in terms of prosecutions  just over 5 000 prosecutions a year do  you think that Target's in reach so I do  so I I absolutely believe it is in reach  and actually you've spoken to even in  Somerset you've seen the integrated work  that's taking place you've seen more  cases go to court but it's in reach for  not just those reasons  um it's in reach because of the support  that's actually going in for women so  you know the independent sexual advisors  the adverse  too long we've seen a lot of Patricia  and you know I used to spend a lot of  time with victims of cryo as a bat bench  MP and you'd see people give up because  of the processes you know long drawn out  processes digital forensics for example  being too intrusive handing over your  mobile phones we've changed that we have  changed that and that is really  important so it is it is Within Reach  and Sarah crew was hopeful it was as  well but  the funding cannot be taken away of  course not of course not the other point  to make as well and is that a message  I'm going to come on to Liz but is that  a message to the government as well  it's in funding so no so so Beth I think  a couple of things here basically we  have been very clear around operation  satira the work that Sarah crew and the  team are doing this this is being rolled  out to other police forces around the  country that has to happen that  absolutely has happened into the fact it  is happening I know it's happening but  also it's that integration across  Criminal Justice System the CPS the  support that goes in from the Grassroots  the community organizations the advers  you know I met them when I last saw  Sarah about six weeks ago these are the  ones that are making a positive impact  and they are the ones who'll hold the  hands of the victims and ensure that  they are supported that money cannot go  and the MOJ are absolutely supporting  that and it's important that we do that  you know when you when you're in  government yes there are plenty of  choices but at the end of the day you  know we have to be on the side of the  victims of crime ensure that we're  supporting them we want to prevent crime  as well obviously but we have to  absolutely ensure that people are  supported lives are devastated through  these awful crimes they really are do  you worry sometimes that some of the  measures that your Administration put in  under Boris Johnson because this is  effectively a new Administration that  they're undermining potentially some of  the work so not seeming you'll talk  about being on the side of victims but  Robert Halford was talking yesterday  about not being on the side of working  people do you worry about that a bit  well look I mean first of all this  government the new government as well  all of us were elected on the 2019  Manifesto and you know my time in  government you know was literally  focused on delivering on Manifesto  commitments it's all about the people  all about the British people you know we  we care about the public I always call  them the people's priorities as did  Boris Johnson as prime minister I  believe in that I absolutely believe in  that that's our duty that's our  responsibility and you know I know I  know my colleagues in government  understand that you didn't run lots of  people thought you might you didn't run  for leadership neither did you come out  in support for either candidate was that  because you didn't much like the look of  either of them well I've always said  that I was I was going to put  literally Party politics to one side  because I had huge responsibilities as  Home Secretary for me the national  security and my constitutional role in  government and I don't want to recount  what happened at the time there's  something it was chaotic over the summer  I think the Public Public saw that I had  a duty to carry on with my job and Beth  you can't be home secretary and then  come out and start joining political  campaigns and things of that nature you  know I've always taken my role seriously  and that was a position that I feel was  right I put I put the interest of the  the country before party quality cabinet  job though I that that for me was not  even a consideration I had the privilege  of serving as Home Secretary and put in  my country first was always a priority  it's been pretty chaotic uh since the  summer as well I have to say you said in  your resignation letter you give Liz  truss your support from the back benches  is she making that hard for you at the  moment well look I've I've said this  she's the leader of our party I was at  conference last week birth and I said  this at conference as well we do have to  come together we've got a Clear Vision  and a clear mandate for the country it's  important that we rally behind that and  just start delivering it let me put it  another way and I won't keep pressing  you but a clear Monday for the country  is she muddy in the waters by  introducing policies not agreed by  cabinet not in the manifesto and that